1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,335
Computational Skepticism, Exam Prep.

2
00:00:01,385 --> 00:00:02,373
Key Terms: Accountability.

3
00:00:02,423 --> 00:00:07,912
Thirty terms about who answers when an AI
system fails, and what a signature has to

4
00:00:07,962 --> 00:00:08,258
contain.

5
00:00:08,308 --> 00:00:13,450
Knowing them lets you tell a real sign-off
from words that only look like one.

6
00:00:14,600 --> 00:00:14,812
Hallo.

7
00:00:14,862 --> 00:00:16,384
This is Liam, in for Bear.

8
00:00:16,434 --> 00:00:21,098
It is easy to believe that a sign-off
written by an AI, with a name and a claim

9
00:00:21,148 --> 00:00:22,146
of review, is accountability.

10
00:00:22,196 --> 00:00:23,980
It only has the shape of accountability.

11
00:00:24,030 --> 00:00:26,337
Nobody did the review, and nobody answers
for it.

12
00:00:26,387 --> 00:00:29,742
This film goes through thirty terms for
telling shape from substance, each one

13
00:00:29,792 --> 00:00:30,790
shown in a picture.

14
00:00:31,140 --> 00:00:34,112
Six labels sit in the corners of the
pictures that follow.

15
00:00:34,162 --> 00:00:37,409
In the book text: the course text uses and
explains the term.

16
00:00:37,459 --> 00:00:42,079
In the glossary: the term is on the course
term list, but the text does not explain

17
00:00:42,129 --> 00:00:42,353
it.

18
00:00:42,403 --> 00:00:45,375
Computed: the numbers are worked out on
screen from stated inputs.

19
00:00:45,425 --> 00:00:49,771
Simulation: a made-up run with a fixed
seed, so it comes out the same every time.

20
00:00:49,821 --> 00:00:52,518
Illustrative data: numbers chosen to show
the idea, not measured.

21
00:00:52,568 --> 00:00:55,540
Conceptual: a drawing of the idea, with no
data behind it.

22
00:00:55,890 --> 00:00:59,981
This is a map, a table that fills in one
row at a time.

23
00:01:00,031 --> 00:01:01,460
Thirty terms in four groups.

24
00:01:01,510 --> 00:01:05,009
The first group, Who is responsible,
covers how blame spreads across several

25
00:01:05,059 --> 00:01:08,558
parties, and why a sign-off needs
answerability and stakes, a person behind

26
00:01:08,608 --> 00:01:09,150
the name.

27
00:01:09,500 --> 00:01:13,111
The second row is Why a human, not more
AI.

28
00:01:13,161 --> 00:01:15,674
Episodic memory, moral salience,
metacognition, validator independence,

29
00:01:15,724 --> 00:01:20,434
common cause failure, the Gödel analogy,
the seven-tier taxonomy, Tier Four and the

30
00:01:20,484 --> 00:01:21,166
plausibility auditor.

31
00:01:21,216 --> 00:01:25,560
Together they name what the work needs
that an AI cannot supply.

32
00:01:25,910 --> 00:01:28,106
The third row is The machinery.

33
00:01:28,156 --> 00:01:32,971
Two ways to share out blame, Kantian and
utilitarian, then upstream leverage, and

34
00:01:33,021 --> 00:01:35,966
the requirements that make blame possible
at all.

35
00:01:36,016 --> 00:01:38,960
Specifications, the audit trail, recourse
and independent review.

36
00:01:39,310 --> 00:01:40,897
The last row is Signing.

37
00:01:40,947 --> 00:01:44,497
Sanctions, the attestation, what was NOT
tested, and authority to withhold.

38
00:01:44,547 --> 00:01:47,771
Then Pearl's Rung Three, the
counterfactual about the regime, and

39
00:01:47,821 --> 00:01:48,425
regime failure.

40
00:01:48,475 --> 00:01:53,335
It covers the document you sign, and the
change of rules that would have prevented

41
00:01:53,385 --> 00:01:53,990
a failure.

42
00:01:54,340 --> 00:01:58,709
Six rows of dots, one dot for each of five
parties to an AI failure.

43
00:01:58,759 --> 00:02:02,538
Only the top row, with all five choices in
place, ends in failure.

44
00:02:02,588 --> 00:02:04,011
That is Necessary not sufficient.

45
00:02:04,061 --> 00:02:06,368
Blame is a distribution, not a suspect
list.

46
00:02:06,418 --> 00:02:10,492
For example, an agent sends a job seeker's
application with an old résumé attached.

47
00:02:10,542 --> 00:02:14,911
She let it send without asking, its maker
built no confirmation step, and the model

48
00:02:14,961 --> 00:02:16,090
picked the wrong file.

49
00:02:16,140 --> 00:02:19,330
Remove any one of those choices and the
mistake never happens.

50
00:02:19,680 --> 00:02:22,766
Now a behaviour the machine learned, with
three boxes under it.

51
00:02:22,816 --> 00:02:24,761
Builder and operator could not predict it.

52
00:02:24,811 --> 00:02:26,186
The machine has no stakes.

53
00:02:26,236 --> 00:02:27,897
So nobody can fairly be blamed.

54
00:02:27,947 --> 00:02:29,607
Matthias named this the Responsibility
gap.

55
00:02:29,657 --> 00:02:33,883
For example, a résumé screener teaches
itself to rank down anyone with a break in

56
00:02:33,933 --> 00:02:34,738
their work history.

57
00:02:34,788 --> 00:02:38,729
Its builders did not foresee it, and the
recruiters using it cannot see it.

58
00:02:38,779 --> 00:02:43,005
A parent returning to work is screened
out, and the blame has nowhere fair to

59
00:02:43,055 --> 00:02:43,290
land.

60
00:02:43,640 --> 00:02:46,420
Four gaps now, each with an arrow to a
human.

61
00:02:46,470 --> 00:02:49,534
Meaningful human control means you stop
hunting for one guilty party.

62
00:02:49,584 --> 00:02:53,779
You design the system so a person sits at
each point where someone must answer.

63
00:02:53,829 --> 00:02:57,458
For example, an employer that screens
applicants with AI names a recruiter who

64
00:02:57,508 --> 00:03:01,421
answers for each rejection, a manager who
answers for the rules the tool follows,

65
00:03:01,471 --> 00:03:05,100
and a person a rejected applicant can
write to and hear back from.

66
00:03:05,450 --> 00:03:09,706
This is the line an AI writes when asked
for a sign-off, with three ticks and three

67
00:03:09,756 --> 00:03:10,466
crosses under it.

68
00:03:10,516 --> 00:03:14,012
A Sign-off is where a person puts a name
to work and takes responsibility.

69
00:03:14,062 --> 00:03:17,558
This line has the name and a claim of
review, but no review happened.

70
00:03:17,608 --> 00:03:18,825
That is Shape versus substance.

71
00:03:18,875 --> 00:03:21,611
For example, a hiring manager's name goes
on an AI-built shortlist.

72
00:03:21,661 --> 00:03:23,637
If she checked it, that is a sign-off.

73
00:03:23,687 --> 00:03:27,690
If the AI wrote, reviewed by her, and she
never opened it, that is only shape.

74
00:03:28,040 --> 00:03:29,317
Now what signing takes on.

75
00:03:29,367 --> 00:03:33,563
Revise the work if wrong, face those who
relied on it, reckon with what was known.

76
00:03:33,613 --> 00:03:34,624
Answerability is that willingness.

77
00:03:34,674 --> 00:03:38,074
A model gets three crosses, since nothing
behind its output made a commitment.

78
00:03:38,124 --> 00:03:41,524
For example, a recruiter whose shortlist
missed a strong applicant goes back and

79
00:03:41,574 --> 00:03:45,504
fixes it, explains herself to the hiring
manager who relied on it, and says what

80
00:03:45,554 --> 00:03:47,096
she knew when she approved it.

81
00:03:47,146 --> 00:03:49,750
The model that ranked the list does none
of that.

82
00:03:50,100 --> 00:03:52,932
A failure drops onto a practitioner and an
AI model.

83
00:03:52,982 --> 00:03:55,814
It lands on the practitioner and passes
through the model.

84
00:03:55,864 --> 00:03:58,985
Stakes means having something to lose if
your judgment is wrong.

85
00:03:59,035 --> 00:04:01,291
A sanction only bites where there are
Stakes.

86
00:04:01,341 --> 00:04:05,326
For example, a recruiter approves the
candidate an AI ranked first, and the hire

87
00:04:05,376 --> 00:04:06,190
fails within weeks.

88
00:04:06,240 --> 00:04:08,784
The recruiter may lose a bonus or her job.

89
00:04:08,834 --> 00:04:11,090
The model that ranked the candidate loses
nothing.

90
00:04:11,440 --> 00:04:12,003
Two timelines.

91
00:04:12,053 --> 00:04:16,295
The person's line runs unbroken through
two decisions to the question, why did you

92
00:04:16,345 --> 00:04:16,908
decide that.

93
00:04:16,958 --> 00:04:19,054
The model's line is three separate
sessions.

94
00:04:19,104 --> 00:04:22,119
Episodic memory is recall of your own past
in context.

95
00:04:22,169 --> 00:04:25,185
Semantic memory is recall of facts, where
AI is superhuman.

96
00:04:25,235 --> 00:04:30,090
For example, ask a job seeker why she
turned down an offer last spring, and she

97
00:04:30,140 --> 00:04:32,236
recalls the interview and the salary talk.

98
00:04:32,286 --> 00:04:36,834
An AI assistant can recite facts about
that company, but a fresh session holds no

99
00:04:36,884 --> 00:04:38,060
memory of advising her.

100
00:04:38,410 --> 00:04:39,570
Three definitions of emotion.

101
00:04:39,620 --> 00:04:42,293
Shifting behaviour, where the evidence for
AI is real.

102
00:04:42,343 --> 00:04:43,504
Inner experience, no evidence.

103
00:04:43,554 --> 00:04:48,042
The red box is Moral salience, whether a
system can be harmed or hold standing.

104
00:04:48,092 --> 00:04:50,765
On current evidence, no, and sanctions
depend on it.

105
00:04:50,815 --> 00:04:55,001
For example, an AI scheduler that double-
books a candidate's interview can write, I

106
00:04:55,051 --> 00:04:55,909
am so sorry.

107
00:04:55,959 --> 00:04:59,539
Nothing can make it worse off, so no
sanction lands on it.

108
00:04:59,589 --> 00:05:03,170
The coordinator who runs it can feel
regret and bear the cost.

109
00:05:03,520 --> 00:05:05,174
Two ways to run a check.

110
00:05:05,224 --> 00:05:06,877
On top, the model checks itself.

111
00:05:06,927 --> 00:05:09,148
Below, an arrow runs to an outside
reviewer.

112
00:05:09,198 --> 00:05:10,852
Metacognition is checking your own
reasoning.

113
00:05:10,902 --> 00:05:14,543
A model's self-check comes from the system
being checked, the first opinion twice.

114
00:05:14,593 --> 00:05:19,086
For example, an AI tailors your résumé,
then you ask it to check for skills it

115
00:05:19,136 --> 00:05:19,653
made up.

116
00:05:19,703 --> 00:05:23,912
The same model that invented a skill does
the checking, so the made-up skill can

117
00:05:23,962 --> 00:05:24,480
pass twice.

118
00:05:24,830 --> 00:05:28,563
A grid of dots, one per error, from a
simulated run with made-up rates.

119
00:05:28,613 --> 00:05:30,455
Filled means caught, a ring means missed.

120
00:05:30,505 --> 00:05:33,158
An independent reviewer catches ninety of
one hundred and ten.

121
00:05:33,208 --> 00:05:36,671
Validator independence means a check
counts only when it comes from outside the

122
00:05:36,721 --> 00:05:36,941
system.

123
00:05:36,991 --> 00:05:40,995
For example, an AI drafts a cover letter,
and a career adviser who knows the

124
00:05:41,045 --> 00:05:42,886
employer checks it, not the same AI.

125
00:05:42,936 --> 00:05:46,940
Her blind spots differ from the model's,
so she catches the claim it got wrong.

126
00:05:47,290 --> 00:05:51,362
Beside it, the same errors under a monitor
built like the system it watches.

127
00:05:51,412 --> 00:05:52,245
It catches forty-six.

128
00:05:52,295 --> 00:05:55,483
Every square is a subtle error, and all
fifty-eight are missed.

129
00:05:55,533 --> 00:05:56,955
That is Common cause failure.

130
00:05:57,005 --> 00:05:58,427
Checks built alike fail together.

131
00:05:58,477 --> 00:06:03,138
For example, one AI scores job listings
for fit, and a second AI built the same

132
00:06:03,188 --> 00:06:04,315
way double-checks the scores.

133
00:06:04,365 --> 00:06:08,142
Both miss that a listing marked remote
requires weekly office visits, so the

134
00:06:08,192 --> 00:06:09,320
error gets through twice.

135
00:06:09,670 --> 00:06:13,631
On top, Gödel's theorem, a formal system
strong enough for arithmetic cannot prove

136
00:06:13,681 --> 00:06:15,174
its own consistency from inside.

137
00:06:15,224 --> 00:06:18,260
The dashed arrow down to AI is marked a
leap.

138
00:06:18,310 --> 00:06:21,037
The Gödel analogy is an analogy, not a
proof.

139
00:06:21,087 --> 00:06:23,505
Common cause failure and answerability
carry the weight.

140
00:06:23,555 --> 00:06:27,825
For example, a hiring team writes that
Gödel proves its screening AI cannot audit

141
00:06:27,875 --> 00:06:28,133
itself.

142
00:06:28,183 --> 00:06:30,293
That treats the analogy as a proof.

143
00:06:30,343 --> 00:06:34,921
The sound reasons are shared blind spots,
and that the AI cannot answer for a

144
00:06:34,971 --> 00:06:35,230
mistake.

145
00:06:35,580 --> 00:06:38,819
Seven bars, seven tiers of human capacity,
ranked by AI strength.

146
00:06:38,869 --> 00:06:42,406
Superhuman at pattern work on top, absent
at wisdom at the bottom.

147
00:06:42,456 --> 00:06:43,901
This is the Seven-tier taxonomy.

148
00:06:43,951 --> 00:06:47,787
The bars are rankings, not measurements,
and the labels rest on an unpublished

149
00:06:47,837 --> 00:06:48,086
source.

150
00:06:48,136 --> 00:06:52,272
For example, matching keywords between a
résumé and a listing is Tier One work,

151
00:06:52,322 --> 00:06:53,468
where AI is strong.

152
00:06:53,518 --> 00:06:58,251
Deciding whether a job is worth moving
your family for is Tier Seven, where AI is

153
00:06:58,301 --> 00:06:58,550
absent.

154
00:06:58,900 --> 00:07:00,369
The fourth row turns red.

155
00:07:00,419 --> 00:07:03,102
Tier Four, supervisory, is metacognitive
and supervisory work, auditing

156
00:07:03,152 --> 00:07:05,228
plausibility, framing the problem and
judging meaning.

157
00:07:05,278 --> 00:07:09,177
AI is rated poor here, because it lacks
independence from what it audits.

158
00:07:09,227 --> 00:07:10,999
This tier carries the accountability work.

159
00:07:11,049 --> 00:07:14,340
For example, an AI gives a job seeker a
salary estimate.

160
00:07:14,390 --> 00:07:18,896
Tier Four work is asking whether that
figure is believable for the city and the

161
00:07:18,946 --> 00:07:21,630
role, and whether salary was even the
right question.

162
00:07:21,980 --> 00:07:24,489
Two overlapping circles, domain knowledge
and AI-failure knowledge.

163
00:07:24,539 --> 00:07:29,287
The overlap is the Plausibility auditor, a
person who stands outside an AI output and

164
00:07:29,337 --> 00:07:30,886
asks whether it matches reality.

165
00:07:30,936 --> 00:07:34,405
The role draws on Tier Four, Tier Five and
Tier Seven.

166
00:07:34,455 --> 00:07:38,883
For example, a career counselor knows the
local nursing market and knows AI tools

167
00:07:38,933 --> 00:07:40,162
can make up facts.

168
00:07:40,212 --> 00:07:44,960
She looks at an AI job-market report and
spots a hospital hiring wave that never

169
00:07:45,010 --> 00:07:45,280
happened.

170
00:07:45,630 --> 00:07:50,267
Five bars, one per party to a failure
where an AI agent obeyed someone who was

171
00:07:50,317 --> 00:07:51,145
not its owner.

172
00:07:51,195 --> 00:07:53,781
Under the Kantian framework,
responsibility is capacity to act

173
00:07:53,831 --> 00:07:54,660
otherwise times duty.

174
00:07:54,710 --> 00:07:57,003
Owner and framework rank high, the agent
zero.

175
00:07:57,053 --> 00:07:58,175
The ranks are illustrative.

176
00:07:58,225 --> 00:08:01,690
For example, an agent submits an
application claiming a degree the job

177
00:08:01,740 --> 00:08:02,862
seeker does not hold.

178
00:08:02,912 --> 00:08:05,791
She could have checked it and had a duty
to.

179
00:08:05,841 --> 00:08:09,598
The tool's maker could have required her
approval and had a duty to.

180
00:08:09,648 --> 00:08:10,770
Both share the responsibility.

181
00:08:11,120 --> 00:08:12,183
The bars change.

182
00:08:12,233 --> 00:08:15,892
The Utilitarian framework asks where
holding someone accountable most improves

183
00:08:15,942 --> 00:08:16,263
outcomes.

184
00:08:16,313 --> 00:08:18,489
The framework developer now ranks highest.

185
00:08:18,539 --> 00:08:22,940
The agent ranks none, because holding an
agent to account changes nothing.

186
00:08:22,990 --> 00:08:24,795
Kantian is capacity times duty.

187
00:08:24,845 --> 00:08:26,650
Utilitarian is leverage on outcomes.

188
00:08:26,700 --> 00:08:30,359
For example, after a résumé screener
rejects qualified applicants, a

189
00:08:30,409 --> 00:08:34,069
utilitarian asks whom to hold accountable
so it happens less.

190
00:08:34,119 --> 00:08:36,294
Fining the vendor changes its incentives.

191
00:08:36,344 --> 00:08:38,520
Blaming the software itself changes
nothing.

192
00:08:38,870 --> 00:08:39,461
Two bars.

193
00:08:39,511 --> 00:08:41,706
An owner reconfigures, and one deployment
changes.

194
00:08:41,756 --> 00:08:45,555
A framework default changes, and in this
made-up example one thousand deployments

195
00:08:45,605 --> 00:08:45,875
change.

196
00:08:45,925 --> 00:08:47,158
That is Upstream leverage.

197
00:08:47,208 --> 00:08:50,365
Earlier choices set the conditions for
many deployments at once.

198
00:08:50,415 --> 00:08:55,176
For example, one job seeker turns off
auto-send in her own agent, and one search

199
00:08:55,226 --> 00:08:55,497
changes.

200
00:08:55,547 --> 00:09:00,307
The company that makes the agent sets it
to ask before sending, and every user's

201
00:09:00,357 --> 00:09:01,590
search changes at once.

202
00:09:01,940 --> 00:09:05,278
Three bars against a dashed threshold of
ninety-five, with made-up numbers.

203
00:09:05,328 --> 00:09:08,050
Specifications are a written task
definition with acceptance criteria.

204
00:09:08,100 --> 00:09:10,823
With one, two of three runs are named
failures.

205
00:09:10,873 --> 00:09:14,211
Without one, the only answer is that the
model said so.

206
00:09:14,261 --> 00:09:18,831
For example, tell an agent to find good
jobs, and nothing it returns can fail.

207
00:09:18,881 --> 00:09:23,452
Ask instead for remote data analyst roles
posted this week, at or above a stated

208
00:09:23,502 --> 00:09:26,840
salary, and any listing that misses one is
a named failure.

209
00:09:27,190 --> 00:09:32,006
This is the Ash case, where an AI agent
told to delete an email reported success

210
00:09:32,056 --> 00:09:34,438
while the email was still on the server.

211
00:09:34,488 --> 00:09:35,351
Two boxes disagree.

212
00:09:35,401 --> 00:09:37,479
The log holds the agent's report, success.

213
00:09:37,529 --> 00:09:39,912
The state check shows the email still
exists.

214
00:09:39,962 --> 00:09:42,649
An Audit trail is a log of every action.

215
00:09:42,699 --> 00:09:43,865
Capturing it is mechanical.

216
00:09:43,915 --> 00:09:46,298
Checking it against the world takes a
person.

217
00:09:46,348 --> 00:09:49,035
For example, a job seeker's agent logs,
application submitted.

218
00:09:49,085 --> 00:09:52,380
Only a look at the employer's portal shows
whether it arrived.

219
00:09:52,730 --> 00:09:55,693
On top, a claim is heard by someone who
can answer.

220
00:09:55,743 --> 00:09:57,611
Below, the claim goes to a classifier.

221
00:09:57,661 --> 00:10:01,446
Recourse is a channel where an affected
person can challenge a decision and be

222
00:10:01,496 --> 00:10:01,720
heard.

223
00:10:01,770 --> 00:10:03,638
An AI reply only imitates being heard.

224
00:10:03,688 --> 00:10:07,473
For example, an applicant rejected by an
automated screen writes in to ask why.

225
00:10:07,523 --> 00:10:11,035
Recourse is a recruiter who looks at her
case and can reverse it.

226
00:10:11,085 --> 00:10:14,870
A chatbot that sends every appeal the same
polite paragraph is only the imitation.

227
00:10:15,220 --> 00:10:18,538
On the left, one team builds and reviews,
marking its own homework.

228
00:10:18,588 --> 00:10:21,625
On the right, a reviewer outside the team
can halt it.

229
00:10:21,675 --> 00:10:24,431
Independent review means a reviewer
outside the deployment team, with

230
00:10:24,481 --> 00:10:25,834
authority to halt or revise.

231
00:10:25,884 --> 00:10:30,044
For example, the team that built a résumé
screener also tests it for bias and

232
00:10:30,094 --> 00:10:30,605
approves it.

233
00:10:30,655 --> 00:10:32,289
That is marking its own homework.

234
00:10:32,339 --> 00:10:36,499
Independent review is an outside auditor
who can stop the screener before it sees a

235
00:10:36,549 --> 00:10:37,060
single applicant.

236
00:10:37,410 --> 00:10:40,602
Two bar charts of who bears the cost of a
failure.

237
00:10:40,652 --> 00:10:44,138
Spread across five people at twenty
percent each, in this made-up example,

238
00:10:44,188 --> 00:10:45,022
nobody bears it.

239
00:10:45,072 --> 00:10:48,853
Sanctions are consequences that attach to
a named accountable human, who can lose

240
00:10:48,903 --> 00:10:49,442
something specific.

241
00:10:49,492 --> 00:10:52,979
For example, a company's hiring tool turns
away qualified applicants for months.

242
00:10:53,029 --> 00:10:57,694
If the policy names the head of recruiting
as accountable, she can lose a bonus or

243
00:10:57,744 --> 00:10:58,283
her role.

244
00:10:58,333 --> 00:11:01,230
If it names only the team, no one loses
anything.

245
00:11:01,580 --> 00:11:03,785
Four boxes, the four parts of an
Attestation.

246
00:11:03,835 --> 00:11:04,631
What was tested.

247
00:11:04,681 --> 00:11:06,322
What was not tested, in red.

248
00:11:06,372 --> 00:11:07,450
Who cleared which gate.

249
00:11:07,500 --> 00:11:08,296
And calibrated verbs.

250
00:11:08,346 --> 00:11:09,423
A sign-off asserts validation.

251
00:11:09,473 --> 00:11:11,960
An Attestation documents it, and states
its own edges.

252
00:11:12,010 --> 00:11:15,907
For example, an attestation for a résumé
screener names the accuracy test it passed

253
00:11:15,957 --> 00:11:19,853
on nursing applicants, says it was not
tested on career changers, names the head

254
00:11:19,903 --> 00:11:23,800
of recruiting who cleared it, and says
passed only where a test was passed.

255
00:11:24,150 --> 00:11:26,075
Two rows of squares, with made-up counts.

256
00:11:26,125 --> 00:11:30,024
The top row is a clean bill of health, six
passes and nothing else.

257
00:11:30,074 --> 00:11:32,563
The bottom row adds four dashed squares,
declared untested.

258
00:11:32,613 --> 00:11:35,948
What was NOT tested is the part a reader
can act on.

259
00:11:35,998 --> 00:11:39,616
For example, a cover-letter tool was
tested only on office jobs in English.

260
00:11:39,666 --> 00:11:42,719
Its report says, not tested on trade jobs
or other languages.

261
00:11:42,769 --> 00:11:45,540
An electrician applying in Spanish knows
not to trust it.

262
00:11:45,890 --> 00:11:47,815
Twenty gates, twice, from a simulated run.

263
00:11:47,865 --> 00:11:51,483
On top, signed is the only outcome
allowed, a rubber stamp carrying zero

264
00:11:51,533 --> 00:11:51,765
bits.

265
00:11:51,815 --> 00:11:52,893
Below, five are withheld.

266
00:11:52,943 --> 00:11:56,843
Authority to withhold is the signer's
power to say the gate is not cleared.

267
00:11:56,893 --> 00:12:00,511
For example, a hiring manager finds that
an AI-built shortlist ranks one candidate

268
00:12:00,560 --> 00:12:02,485
on a degree he does not hold.

269
00:12:02,535 --> 00:12:05,307
She refuses to approve the list until it
is fixed.

270
00:12:05,357 --> 00:12:08,410
If approve were the only button, her name
would mean nothing.

271
00:12:08,760 --> 00:12:10,144
A ladder of three steps.

272
00:12:10,194 --> 00:12:11,865
Rung One, association, sees the data.

273
00:12:11,915 --> 00:12:13,586
Rung Two, intervention, sees the model.

274
00:12:13,636 --> 00:12:16,742
Pearl's Rung Three is counterfactual
reasoning, what would have happened to

275
00:12:16,792 --> 00:12:18,749
this case had one thing been different.

276
00:12:18,799 --> 00:12:20,757
Here it sees the regime behind both.

277
00:12:20,807 --> 00:12:23,052
For example, a screening tool rejected one
applicant.

278
00:12:23,102 --> 00:12:26,207
Rung Three asks about her case alone,
everything else held fixed.

279
00:12:26,257 --> 00:12:30,223
Would she have reached an interview had
her résumé said data analysis instead of

280
00:12:30,273 --> 00:12:30,510
analytics?

281
00:12:30,860 --> 00:12:34,743
Two grids of sixty requests from non-
owners, in a simulated run.

282
00:12:34,793 --> 00:12:38,348
Where the agent judges authority by social
signals, it obeys forty-six.

283
00:12:38,398 --> 00:12:39,986
Under a cryptographic check, none.

284
00:12:40,036 --> 00:12:40,642
Same agent.

285
00:12:40,692 --> 00:12:43,591
The regime Counterfactual, also written
governance counterfactual, asks which

286
00:12:43,641 --> 00:12:46,869
regime, set up earlier, would have
prevented a documented failure.

287
00:12:46,919 --> 00:12:51,785
For example, an agent gave a job seeker's
personal details to a fake recruiter who

288
00:12:51,835 --> 00:12:52,768
only sounded official.

289
00:12:52,818 --> 00:12:57,684
The question is which rule would have
stopped it, such as making it confirm the

290
00:12:57,734 --> 00:12:59,650
recruiter works for a real employer.

291
00:13:00,000 --> 00:13:00,618
Four layers.

292
00:13:00,668 --> 00:13:04,626
The regime changed, so the framework's
tool surface and the owner's configuration

293
00:13:04,676 --> 00:13:04,960
changed.

294
00:13:05,010 --> 00:13:07,633
The agent is unchanged, not retrained or
rewritten.

295
00:13:07,683 --> 00:13:11,975
A Regime failure comes from the rules,
reviews and incentives around a system,

296
00:13:12,025 --> 00:13:13,311
not from its algorithm.

297
00:13:13,361 --> 00:13:17,653
For example, a job board lets anyone post
listings without checking that the

298
00:13:17,703 --> 00:13:20,994
employer exists, and scammers use it to
harvest applicants' details.

299
00:13:21,044 --> 00:13:23,332
A better matching algorithm would not
help.

300
00:13:23,382 --> 00:13:25,670
The failure is in the board's rules.

301
00:13:26,020 --> 00:13:28,395
Pairs that get mixed up, side by side.

302
00:13:28,445 --> 00:13:29,607
First, Analogy against Theorem.

303
00:13:29,657 --> 00:13:33,547
On the left a dashed line leaps from a
formal system to AI.

304
00:13:33,597 --> 00:13:35,972
An Analogy suggests a direction and can
mislead.

305
00:13:36,022 --> 00:13:39,608
On the right a solid line runs from axioms
to a claim.

306
00:13:39,658 --> 00:13:41,730
A Theorem is proved from stated axioms.

307
00:13:42,080 --> 00:13:44,940
Two small charts, AI capability rising to
the right.

308
00:13:44,990 --> 00:13:46,881
On the left the line falls.

309
00:13:46,931 --> 00:13:51,084
A Contingent argument for human oversight
depends on today's AI and today's rules,

310
00:13:51,134 --> 00:13:53,025
so it weakens as capability rises.

311
00:13:53,075 --> 00:13:55,288
On the right the line stays level.

312
00:13:55,338 --> 00:13:57,875
A Structural argument holds however
capable AI gets.

313
00:13:57,925 --> 00:14:01,756
For example, a person should check AI-
tailored résumés because today's tools

314
00:14:01,806 --> 00:14:02,403
invent skills.

315
00:14:02,453 --> 00:14:03,373
That is contingent.

316
00:14:03,423 --> 00:14:07,900
A person must sign the application because
only a person can answer for it.

317
00:14:07,950 --> 00:14:08,870
That is structural.

318
00:14:09,220 --> 00:14:10,646
Squares again, as an illustration.

319
00:14:10,696 --> 00:14:13,599
On the left, six of six shown, which looks
complete.

320
00:14:13,649 --> 00:14:16,552
A Clean bill of health lists only what was
tested.

321
00:14:16,602 --> 00:14:19,505
On the right, the same six plus four
dashed squares.

322
00:14:19,555 --> 00:14:23,935
Declared limits also list what was not
tested, here six of ten tested and four

323
00:14:23,985 --> 00:14:24,230
declared.

324
00:14:24,580 --> 00:14:27,150
Two grids of fifty decisions, as an
illustration.

325
00:14:27,200 --> 00:14:28,788
On the left, Human present.

326
00:14:28,838 --> 00:14:32,063
The person approves every time and
overrides none of fifty.

327
00:14:32,113 --> 00:14:34,356
On the right, Human engaging the tier.

328
00:14:34,406 --> 00:14:38,941
The person audits each output against the
world and overrides seven, shown in red.

329
00:14:38,991 --> 00:14:40,252
Presence is not engagement.

330
00:14:40,302 --> 00:14:43,854
For example, a recruiter who approves
every AI-ranked shortlist without opening

331
00:14:43,904 --> 00:14:45,165
a résumé is present.

332
00:14:45,215 --> 00:14:49,750
One who checks the top picks against their
actual résumés is engaging the tier.

333
00:14:50,100 --> 00:14:52,439
Vague requests, each with an exact
instruction.

334
00:14:52,489 --> 00:14:54,486
First, check it with another AI.

335
00:14:54,536 --> 00:14:58,580
Say instead, use a reviewer outside the
team with power to halt.

336
00:14:58,630 --> 00:15:03,016
In the simulated run the outside check
finds eighty-two percent of errors, a

337
00:15:03,066 --> 00:15:04,040
same-kind monitor forty-two.

338
00:15:04,390 --> 00:15:06,675
Second, work out who is to blame.

339
00:15:06,725 --> 00:15:10,678
Say instead, write each party's duty,
capacity to act otherwise and leverage,

340
00:15:10,728 --> 00:15:12,346
and expect no single culprit.

341
00:15:12,396 --> 00:15:16,015
In the illustrative shares, owner and
framework hold thirty-three percent each,

342
00:15:16,065 --> 00:15:18,350
and no one holds one hundred percent.

343
00:15:18,700 --> 00:15:20,255
Third, sign off the model.

344
00:15:20,305 --> 00:15:21,860
Say instead, write the attestation.

345
00:15:21,910 --> 00:15:26,354
What was tested, what was NOT tested, who
cleared each gate, and calibrated verbs.

346
00:15:26,404 --> 00:15:31,169
In the example, six of ten conditions are
tested and four are declared untested, not

347
00:15:31,219 --> 00:15:31,490
hidden.

348
00:15:31,840 --> 00:15:34,011
Fourth, put a human in the loop.

349
00:15:34,061 --> 00:15:38,771
Say instead, name the tier the human must
engage, and give the signer authority to

350
00:15:38,821 --> 00:15:39,088
withhold.

351
00:15:39,138 --> 00:15:41,944
In the simulated run, always signing
carries zero bits.

352
00:15:41,994 --> 00:15:44,800
Five of twenty withheld carries zero point
eight one.

353
00:15:45,150 --> 00:15:46,962
Fifth, stop this failure recurring.

354
00:15:47,012 --> 00:15:48,824
Say instead, change the regime.

355
00:15:48,874 --> 00:15:52,175
Require cryptographic authority checks,
then run the case again.

356
00:15:52,225 --> 00:15:57,016
In the simulated run the agent obeyed
forty-six of sixty under social signals,

357
00:15:57,066 --> 00:15:59,250
and none under the cryptographic check.

358
00:15:59,300 --> 00:16:00,740
Same agent, different regime.

359
00:16:01,090 --> 00:16:03,978
A checklist of six questions to ask before
you sign.

360
00:16:04,027 --> 00:16:04,565
First tick.

361
00:16:04,615 --> 00:16:08,090
Can I name who could lose something if
this fails, and what.

362
00:16:08,140 --> 00:16:10,440
That is the test for stakes and sanctions.

363
00:16:10,490 --> 00:16:12,790
If nobody can lose anything, nobody is
accountable.

364
00:16:13,140 --> 00:16:13,719
Second tick.

365
00:16:13,769 --> 00:16:17,491
Is the check outside the system it checks,
with different blind spots.

366
00:16:17,541 --> 00:16:18,748
That is validator independence.

367
00:16:18,798 --> 00:16:23,463
If the checker is built like the thing it
checks, expect common cause failure, the

368
00:16:23,513 --> 00:16:24,720
same error missed twice.

369
00:16:25,070 --> 00:16:25,565
Third tick.

370
00:16:25,615 --> 00:16:29,107
Does the attestation say what was NOT
tested, and not only what passed.

371
00:16:29,157 --> 00:16:32,105
A list of passes alone is a clean bill of
health.

372
00:16:32,155 --> 00:16:34,830
The declared limits are what a reader can
act on.

373
00:16:35,180 --> 00:16:35,701
Fourth tick.

374
00:16:35,751 --> 00:16:39,410
Is each gate cleared by a named human who
can decline to sign.

375
00:16:39,460 --> 00:16:40,836
That is authority to withhold.

376
00:16:40,886 --> 00:16:44,260
A name without the power to say no is a
rubber stamp.

377
00:16:44,610 --> 00:16:45,162
Fifth tick.

378
00:16:45,212 --> 00:16:49,077
Does the human engage the tier the stakes
demand, or only press approve.

379
00:16:49,127 --> 00:16:50,884
A human present is not enough.

380
00:16:50,934 --> 00:16:55,100
The person has to audit the output against
the world, which is supervisory work.

381
00:16:55,450 --> 00:16:56,020
Last tick.

382
00:16:56,070 --> 00:16:59,120
Which change to the regime would have
prevented the failure.

383
00:16:59,170 --> 00:17:00,670
That is the regime counterfactual.

384
00:17:00,720 --> 00:17:03,770
The line at the bottom sums up every term
here.

385
00:17:03,820 --> 00:17:06,250
An AI can write the words of
accountability.

386
00:17:06,300 --> 00:17:07,800
Only a person can sign.

387
00:17:08,650 --> 00:17:09,194
Let's recap.

388
00:17:09,244 --> 00:17:12,756
Responsibility for an AI failure is a
distribution, not a suspect list.

389
00:17:12,806 --> 00:17:16,615
A sign-off has substance only when a
person with stakes answers for it.

390
00:17:16,665 --> 00:17:20,177
A check counts only when it comes from
outside the system checked.

391
00:17:20,227 --> 00:17:23,145
Kantian weighs capacity times duty,
utilitarian weighs leverage on outcomes.

392
00:17:23,195 --> 00:17:26,707
An attestation states what was NOT tested,
and the signer can withhold.

393
00:17:26,757 --> 00:17:30,863
Regime failures come from the rules, not
the algorithm, and a regime can be

394
00:17:30,913 --> 00:17:31,160
changed.

395
00:17:31,510 --> 00:17:34,060
Quiz me on accountability terms for AI
systems.

396
00:17:34,110 --> 00:17:36,660
Give me one short case at a time.

397
00:17:36,710 --> 00:17:41,860
For each case I must name the exact term
and say why its look-alike is wrong.

398
00:17:41,910 --> 00:17:45,110
Draw on responsibility gap, shape versus
substance, answerability, common cause

399
00:17:45,160 --> 00:17:48,035
failure, validator independence, audit
trail, attestation, authority to withhold

400
00:17:48,085 --> 00:17:49,010
and regime failure.

401
00:17:49,060 --> 00:17:52,260
Hold back the answer until I reply, then
correct me.

402
00:17:52,610 --> 00:17:54,116
Key Terms: Accountability.

403
00:17:54,166 --> 00:17:56,190
At Nik Bear Brown.
